<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Accounts and payroll</span>
    <h1>Where the month actually went</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:14px;max-width:62ch">
      Fee income on one side, salaries and rent and utilities on the other, and a
      profit figure that is the arithmetic of the two rather than somebody's
      estimate. No accounting training required to read it.
    </p>
    <div class="hero__cta">
      <a class="btn btn--primary btn--lg" href="/pricing" data-app="signup">Start 14 day free trial</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--lg" href="/guide.html">See the handbook chapters</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The expense ledger</h3>
        <p>
          Your own categories: salaries, rent, utilities, repairs, whatever your
          school spends on. A category can be retired but never deleted, because
          deleting one would orphan every old entry filed under it and change
          last year's figures.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Cash drawer sessions</h3>
        <p>
          A collector opens a drawer, takes fees, and closes it by counting what
          is there. A difference between the counted cash and the receipts is not
          rounded away: it is recorded with a written reason, which is the whole
          point of counting.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The day book</h3>
        <p>
          Everything that moved today, in one list, with who entered it. It is the
          screen a principal opens at six o'clock, and the one that makes a
          question about a particular receipt answerable in seconds.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Staff and payroll</h3>
        <p>
          Staff records, salaries, staff attendance with a daily check in code,
          and printable staff cards. Teacher logins are tied to the classes each
          teacher actually teaches, and a leaving date closes the login.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Two honest limits</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:12px;max-width:64ch">
      Because finding out on a Monday morning is worse than reading it here.
    </p>
    <ul class="checklist" style="margin-top:22px">
      <li>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 10.5l4 4 8-9" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <span><b>This is not a general ledger.</b> There is no double entry, no trial balance and no chart of accounts. It answers what came in, what went out and what is left, which is the question a school office actually has.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 10.5l4 4 8-9" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <span><b>The statement is not a bank document.</b> A printed account statement says so at the bottom, because a parent will one day take it to a bank and be turned away, and it is better that the paper says why.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

